WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.670
Frameworks such as MITRE ATT&CK help hunters reason about adversary behavior in a structured way.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.898
ATT&CK organizes tactics, techniques, and procedures.

00:00:07.898 --> 00:00:11.611
Tactics describe the adversary objective, such as persistence, defense evasion,

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:13.839
credential access, discovery, or lateral movement.

00:00:13.839 --> 00:00:16.810
Techniques describe how that objective may be attempted.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:20.523
Procedures describe specific observed ways a technique was carried out.

00:00:20.523 --> 00:00:24.608
Hunters use this structure to turn threat knowledge into defensive questions.

00:00:24.608 --> 00:00:29.435
A key hunting principle is to look for behavior, not only known indicators.

00:00:29.435 --> 00:00:34.634
An indicator of compromise, or IOC, is often a specific value: a file hash,

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:38.347
domain, IP address, email sender, registry path, or command string.

00:00:38.347 --> 00:00:43.546
IOCs can be useful, especially for scoping known activity, but they can change quickly.

00:00:43.546 --> 00:00:46.517
Behavioral evidence focuses on what the activity does.

00:00:46.517 --> 00:00:50.230
For example, encoded command execution, unusual persistence locations, discovery commands,

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:51.344
credential access signals,

00:00:51.344 --> 00:00:57.285
and lateral movement patterns may be visible even when the exact hash or domain is new.

00:00:57.285 --> 00:00:59.885
Behavioral hunting does not require offensive instructions.

00:00:59.885 --> 00:01:04.712
The defender asks what telemetry would show if a category of activity occurred.

00:01:04.712 --> 00:01:07.683
If the question is about encoded command execution,

00:01:07.683 --> 00:01:13.253
the hunter may look for process events where scripting engines launch with suspicious command-line patterns.

00:01:13.253 --> 00:01:15.852
If the question is about unusual persistence,

00:01:15.852 --> 00:01:21.793
the hunter may look for new startup entries or scheduled tasks outside known software deployment tools.

00:01:21.793 --> 00:01:24.393
If the question is about discovery behavior,

00:01:24.393 --> 00:01:29.220
the hunter may look for endpoints issuing many system or directory enumeration commands.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.819
If the question is about lateral movement,

00:01:31.819 --> 00:01:35.533
the hunter may look for unusual remote logons, administrative shares,

00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:37.018
or service creation events.

00:01:37.018 --> 00:01:39.617
Mapping behavior to telemetry prevents shallow searches.

00:01:39.617 --> 00:01:42.588
Instead of searching for one suspicious string everywhere,

00:01:42.588 --> 00:01:47.415
the hunter asks which data source should contain the behavior, which fields matter,

00:01:47.415 --> 00:01:50.015
and which baseline would make it unusual.

00:01:50.015 --> 00:01:52.243
Endpoint telemetry may show process relationships.

00:01:52.243 --> 00:01:54.471
Authentication logs may show account movement.

00:01:54.471 --> 00:01:57.441
DNS and proxy logs may show external communication.

00:01:57.441 --> 00:02:00.041
Cloud control-plane logs may show administrative behavior.

00:02:00.041 --> 00:02:02.640
The mapping links the hypothesis to evidence.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.868
There is still room for IOCs.

00:02:04.868 --> 00:02:08.581
If a credible report names a domain, hash, or certificate,

00:02:08.581 --> 00:02:12.295
a hunter can search for that value to scope exposure.

00:02:12.295 --> 00:02:17.493
The important distinction is that IOC matching often answers whether a known artifact appeared,

00:02:17.493 --> 00:02:22.321
while behavior hunting asks whether a class of suspicious activity may be present.

00:02:22.321 --> 00:02:24.177
Mature hunts often combine both.

00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:27.891
For beginners, ATT&CK is most useful as a thinking aid.

00:02:27.891 --> 00:02:31.604
Do not treat it as a checklist to run mechanically.

00:02:31.604 --> 00:02:35.689
Use it to frame defensible questions, identify likely telemetry, document assumptions,

00:02:35.689 --> 00:02:39.402
and produce detection ideas that survive beyond one temporary indicator.

00:02:39.402 --> 00:02:42.744
Behavior mapping also helps explain findings to other teams.

00:02:42.744 --> 00:02:45.343
Instead of saying a query looked strange,

00:02:45.343 --> 00:02:49.428
the hunter can say the activity aligns with a discovery pattern,

00:02:49.428 --> 00:02:52.770
a credential access signal, or a lateral movement concern,

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:54.998
and then show the supporting telemetry.

00:02:54.998 --> 00:02:58.340
That language connects evidence to risk without overstating certainty.
